    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackTitle">UEFI boot did not start the installer</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackDescription">Windows returned without the live marker. Libertix kept a verified local copy and can use the validated UEFI firmware entry to restart the live system.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootTitle">Secure Boot blocks the recovery fallback</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootDescription">BootNext did not start the live system. The alternate firmware-boot method is not offered because Secure Boot is enabled.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootGuidance">Libertix will first restore the original Windows and UEFI state. After restoration, your PC works normally and you may abandon the Linux installation. Disabling Secure Boot can be complex and differs between computers; ask someone familiar with firmware settings if you are unsure.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootHelpLink">Open Microsoft's English guide to disabling Secure Boot</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootRestoring">Restoring Windows and the original UEFI state...</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootWindowsRestored">Windows and the original UEFI state have been restored. Your PC can be used normally. You may close Libertix and abandon the installation.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="UefiFallbackSecureBootBlockedLog">Secure Boot is enabled; the firmware fallback is disabled.</system:String>
